Aerogel: a tab with a storage partition to itself.

Site isolation, storage partitioning and the ephemeral-sites list all answer
"keep this site away from other sites". None of them answers "keep this site
away from itself" - a second account on a site you are already signed in to,
or a link you would rather not hand to the profile that knows who you are.
Firefox solves that with containers. This is the same idea with a shorter
lifetime: the jar is made when the tab is, and emptied when the tab closes.

The mechanism is content::SiteInstance::CreateForFixedStoragePartition, whose
documented purpose is "a custom StoragePartition that is preserved across
navigations". Plain Create would not do: the first cross-site navigation would
land back in the profile's own partition and the jar would quietly stop being
a jar without anything saying so.

in_memory=true is the load-bearing property. The partition is never written to
disk at all, which is why the clear-on-close below is a statement about memory
rather than a promise to delete something - there is nothing on disk that could
fail to be deleted. It also means the config is honest inside Incognito, where
StoragePartitionConfig::Create forces in_memory on regardless.

What it is NOT is Incognito. History, downloads and the omnibox's memory belong
to the profile and are not partitioned, so an Aerogel tab still records where
it went. This separates identity, not traces. That distinction is why the row
sits beside New Incognito window rather than replacing anything.

Two one-line guards outside our own code, both calling the same predicate:

  * BrowserTabStripModelDelegate::CreateHistoricalTab - so a jar is not
    reopenable. Its partition is emptied as it closes, so "reopen closed tab"
    could not restore the jar; it would hand back a tab that looks identical
    and is signed in as the ordinary profile. That is the exact outcome this
    feature exists to prevent, arriving through the back door.
  * SessionServiceBase::TabInserted - so a jar is never written to the session
    file, for the same reason one restart later.

The helper that makes a tab a jar is also what identifies one: IsJarTab asks
whether the WebContentsUserData is attached, so the two can never disagree, and
it is attached before the tab strip sees the WebContents - session save
observes tab insertion, and attaching afterwards would leave a window in which
a jar looks ordinary and the session file is written from inside it.

Jars go in a tab group titled Aerogel so the tab strip says which tabs are
separate without a new piece of UI existing to say it. One group per window,
reused: two jars are still two separate jars, and the group is a label rather
than a boundary.

Verified against Chromium 152.0.7977.82. Every API this uses was read in the
real headers at that tag rather than recalled - CreateForFixedStoragePartition
and StoragePartitionConfig::Create, the five-argument ClearData whose
documentation says an opaque-origin StorageKey means "all data in this
partition", browser_shutdown::HasShutdownStarted, TabStripModel's
AppendWebContents / AddToNewGroup / AddToExistingGroup / group_model / kNoTab,
TabGroupModel::ListTabGroups and GetTabGroup, TabGroup::visual_data and
SetVisualData, and the TabGroupVisualData constructor. The patch is generated
against a tree carrying the full ungoogled and Aerium series in order, applied
with GNU patch as the build applies it.

Not in this change, deliberately: the link context menu entry ("Open link in
Aerogel tab"), which is the other half of the feature and wants its own pass
over render_view_context_menu.cc, and Android, which has no JNI surface for a
custom StoragePartitionConfig and needs a different approach entirely.

